NAME
       bash - the GNU Bourne-Again shell, the standard command line of RHEL

SYNOPSIS
       bash [-l] [-x] [-n] [-e] [SCRIPT [ARGUMENT...]]
       bash -c COMMANDS [NAME [ARGUMENT...]]

DESCRIPTION
       bash reads command lines, from the keyboard or from a script file, and
       runs them. It is the login shell of new users on RHEL (sh is another
       name for it). This page is a short tour of the parts used most; the
       real reference is much longer.

   Command lines
       A simple command is a program name followed by its arguments,
       separated by spaces. Join commands with:

       A ; B      run A, then B
       A && B     run B only if A succeeded (exit status 0)
       A || B     run B only if A failed
       A | B      send A's output into B's input
       A &        run A in the background (see jobs, fg, bg)

       Every command ends with an exit status: 0 means success, anything
       else a failure. $? holds the status of the last command.

   Quoting
       'text'     nothing inside is changed
       "text"     $variables and $(commands) are expanded, spaces are kept
       \c         the next character loses its special meaning

   Expansion
       $NAME, ${NAME}      the value of a variable
       $(command)          the output of a command
       $((1 + 2))          arithmetic
       *, ?, [abc]         file names that match the pattern
       {a,b}, {1..5}       brace expansion: a b, 1 2 3 4 5
       ~                   your home directory

   Redirection
       > FILE     standard output to FILE (replacing it)
       >> FILE    standard output appended to FILE
       2> FILE    error messages to FILE; 2>/dev/null throws them away
       &> FILE    both output and errors to FILE
       2>&1       errors to wherever output goes now
       < FILE     standard input from FILE
       <<EOF      a here document: the next lines up to EOF are the input

   Variables
       NAME=value sets a variable (no spaces around =). export NAME makes it
       part of the environment of programs started from this shell. Special
       ones in scripts: $1 $2 ... the arguments, $# their number, $@ all of
       them (write "$@"), $0 the script name, $$ the shell's process ID.

   Tests and conditions
       if COMMAND; then ...; elif COMMAND; then ...; else ...; fi
       [ EXPRESSION ] or test EXPRESSION: -f FILE (a regular file), -d (a
       directory), -e (exists), -z STRING (empty), S1 = S2, N1 -eq N2,
       -ne, -lt, -le, -gt, -ge. [[ ... ]] adds pattern matching (==) and
       regular expressions (=~).
       case WORD in PATTERN) ...;; *) ...;; esac

   Loops
       for NAME in WORDS; do ...; done
       for ((i=1; i<=3; i++)); do ...; done
       while COMMAND; do ...; done
       until COMMAND; do ...; done
       while read -r line; do ...; done < FILE
       break and continue leave or restart a loop.

   Scripts
       A script is a text file starting with #!/bin/bash. Make it executable
       (chmod +x) and run it by path (./script.sh), or run bash script.sh.
       exit N ends it with status N.

OPTIONS
       -c COMMANDS
              Run COMMANDS and exit.

       -l, --login
              Act as a login shell (read the login files below).

       -x     Print each command before running it, with a + in front: the
              easiest way to see what a script really does.

       -n     Read the script and check its syntax without running it.

       -e     Stop at the first command that fails.

       -v     Print the lines as they are read.

       --norc, --noprofile
              Do not read the start-up files.

       --version
              Print the version and exit.

EXAMPLES
       Check a script's syntax, then trace it:

           bash -n /root/backup.sh
           bash -x /root/backup.sh

       A script that prints the size of every file given as an argument:

           #!/bin/bash
           if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: $0 FILE..."; exit 1; fi
           for f in "$@"; do
               echo "$f: $(stat -c %s "$f") bytes"
           done

       Run a command line as another user:

           su - student -c 'echo $HOME'

FILES
       /etc/profile, ~/.bash_profile
              Read by login shells.

       /etc/bashrc, ~/.bashrc
              Read by interactive shells (~/.bash_profile usually reads
              ~/.bashrc too). Put aliases and functions here.

       ~/.bash_history
              The commands you typed.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), test(1), env(1), su(1), sudo(8)
